# Entity-first file names, folders as units, modules on the gateway

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 convention.

## Use This When

- You are about to create a file or folder.
- A file becomes a folder of parts (see `file-size-tiers.mdc` for when).
- You add a module to the gateway, or import across two of its modules.

## Stop Here If

- The name reads `<name>.<role>.<ext>` and sits in the layer folder that owns it, and anything imported from another folder-unit or gateway module comes through that folder's `index.ts`.

## Core rule

- File names are `<name>.<role>.<ext>`, the entity or component first: `profile.service.ts`, `profile.view.tsx`, `profile.shape.surql`.
- Fixed roles in a feature: `module`, `shape`, `commands`, `model.gen`, `service`, `machine`, `container`, `view`, `spec`, `view.spec`, `stories`, plus `entity`, `port`, `rule` (one domain rule function, `profile-name.rule.ts`) and `policy` (one application business function, `locale-change.policy.ts`).
- A component folder is kebab-case and has the component's name.
- Feature layout: `<feature>/<feature>.module.ts`, `domain/`, `application/`, `presentation/pages/<page>/`, `presentation/components/<name>/`, `presentation/i18n/`.
- A folder as a unit: when one file grows into several, they live in a folder named by what they share, without repeating that name inside (`region-sync/push.ts`, not `region-sync/region-push.ts`), with an `index.ts` that only re-exports the folder's public surface. Outside the folder, import the folder (`./region-sync`), never a file inside it. No folder for a single file.
- The gateway is built from modules the same way: one folder per mechanism of the platform under `apps/gateway/src/` (`auth/`, `sync/`, `tenants/`, `limits/`), each with an `index.ts` as the only entry point for the rest of the server, its internals private to it, and its own README (`package-readme.mdc`). A module grows `domain/` and `application/` folders once it holds domain rules, not before. The composition root stays the only place that assembles adapters. Modules that predate this rule keep their flat layout until their next split touches them.
- Tests sit next to the code they test.
